          "title": "switch without default can return undefined at runtime",
          "body": "Previously `describe` ended with an unconditional `return \\`key ${...}\\``, so every call returned a string no matter what value arrived at runtime. The new `switch` has only the two literal cases and no `default`, so a value whose `kind` is neither \"click\" nor \"key\" \u2014 e.g. an event decoded from JSON, a network payload, or a third variant added to `Event` by code that is not type-checked against this file \u2014 falls through and the function implicitly returns `undefined`, while its declared return type is still `string`. Callers doing `describe(e).length` or string concatenation would get a TypeError or the literal text \"undefined\". Add a `default:` that either throws or returns a fallback string (an `assertNever(event)` default keeps the compile-time exhaustiveness check and closes the runtime hole).",
